================================================================
ROBOT CAR PROJECT
Hotspot & Web Server Setup Guide
Engineering 3 -- Raspberry Pi Unit
For Raspberry Pi OS Trixie (Debian 13)
================================================================

OVERVIEW
--------
Raspberry Pi OS Trixie uses NetworkManager for all network
configuration. This replaces the older hostapd + dnsmasq + dhcpcd
approach entirely. Setting up a hotspot on Trixie is significantly
simpler -- it takes a handful of commands rather than editing
multiple config files.

  - Part 1: Create the WiFi hotspot using nmcli
  - Part 2: Install Python dependencies and run the Flask server

Estimated time: 15-25 minutes

NOTE: To confirm you are running Trixie, run:
        lsb_release -cs
      It should print: trixie


================================================================
PREREQUISITES
================================================================

Before starting, confirm the following:

  - Raspberry Pi 4B (or 3B+) running Raspberry Pi OS Trixie
  - Pi Camera Module connected to the CSI ribbon port
  - Internet access on the Pi for initial package installation
    (use Ethernet or connect to WiFi temporarily)
  - A laptop or phone with WiFi and a web browser
  - SSH access to the Pi, or a keyboard and monitor attached


================================================================
PART 1 -- CREATE THE WIFI HOTSPOT
================================================================

On Trixie, NetworkManager handles the hotspot entirely. There are
no config files to edit and no extra packages to install. The nmcli
tool does everything in a few commands.

WARNING: If you are connected to the Pi via SSH over WiFi, creating
the hotspot will drop your SSH session -- the Pi's wlan0 interface
switches roles. Connect via Ethernet or attach a keyboard and
monitor before continuing.

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1 -- Create the hotspot connection
----------------------------------------------------------------

Run the following command on the Pi. Replace "RobotCar-Team1" with
your chosen network name and "YourPassword" with a password of at
least 8 characters.

Each line ending with \ is a continuation of the same command:

  sudo nmcli connection add \
    con-name RobotCar-Team1 \
    type wifi \
    ifname wlan0 \
    ssid RobotCar-Team1 \
    802-11-wireless.mode ap \
    802-11-wireless.band bg \
    802-11-wireless.channel 6 \
    wifi-sec.key-mgmt wpa-psk \
    wifi-sec.psk YourPassword \
    wifi-sec.proto rsn \
    wifi-sec.pairwise ccmp \
    ipv4.method shared \
    ipv4.addresses 10.42.0.1/24 \
    connection.autoconnect yes

TIP: Each team should use a unique name (e.g. RobotCar-Team1,
RobotCar-Team2) so hotspots do not conflict in the classroom.
Use different channels too -- 1, 6, and 11 are the three
non-overlapping 2.4 GHz channels.

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 -- Activate the hotspot
----------------------------------------------------------------

  sudo nmcli connection up RobotCar-Team1

The Pi's WiFi will now broadcast the hotspot. On your laptop or
phone, look for the network name you chose in the WiFi list and
connect using the password you set.

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 -- Verify the hotspot is working
----------------------------------------------------------------

On the Pi, confirm the hotspot is active:

  nmcli device status

You should see wlan0 listed with type "wifi" and state "connected".

On the connected laptop, open a browser and go to:

  http://10.42.0.1

You will see a connection error at this point -- that is expected,
because the Flask server has not been started yet. If you cannot
connect to the WiFi network at all, see the Troubleshooting section.

----------------------------------------------------------------
Auto-start on boot
----------------------------------------------------------------

The "connection.autoconnect yes" flag in Step 1 is all that is
needed -- the hotspot starts automatically on every boot.

To confirm autoconnect is enabled:

  nmcli connection show RobotCar-Team1 | grep autoconnect

You should see:   connection.autoconnect: yes

----------------------------------------------------------------
Hotspot settings summary
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Pi IP address  :  10.42.0.1
  DHCP range     :  10.42.0.2 - 10.42.0.254 (assigned by NM)
  Subnet mask    :  255.255.255.0
  WiFi band      :  2.4 GHz (802.11b/g), channel 6
  Security       :  WPA2-PSK (RSN/CCMP)
  Auto-start     :  Yes -- starts on every boot


================================================================
PART 2 -- INSTALL DEPENDENCIES & START THE SERVER
================================================================

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1 -- Install Python packages
----------------------------------------------------------------

The Pi needs internet access for this step. If the hotspot is
already active and there is no Ethernet connection, temporarily
switch back to a WiFi network:

  sudo nmcli connection down RobotCar-Team1
  sudo nmcli device wifi connect YourSchoolWiFi password YourWiFiPassword

Then install the required packages:

  sudo apt update
  sudo apt install -y python3-pip python3-picamera2 python3-flask

If python3-flask is not available via apt, install it with pip:

  sudo pip3 install flask --break-system-packages

NOTE: On Trixie, picamera2 must be installed via apt, not pip.
The apt version is built with the correct system-level libcamera
bindings. Using pip can cause compatibility problems.

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 -- Enable the camera interface
----------------------------------------------------------------

If the camera has not been enabled yet:

  sudo raspi-config

Go to: Interface Options > Camera > Enable. Reboot if prompted.

To quickly test the camera from the terminal:

  libcamera-hello --timeout 2000

This should display a brief camera preview. If it errors, check
the ribbon cable connection.

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 -- Copy the server file to the Pi
----------------------------------------------------------------

If transferring from a laptop on the same network
(replace "pi" with your username if different):

  scp robot_car_server.py pi@10.42.0.1:~/robot_car_server.py

Or create it directly on the Pi:

  nano ~/robot_car_server.py

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 4 -- Restart the hotspot
----------------------------------------------------------------

If you switched to school WiFi for package installation,
bring the hotspot back:

  sudo nmcli connection up RobotCar-Team1

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 5 -- Start the web server
----------------------------------------------------------------

The server uses port 80 (standard HTTP), so sudo is required:

  sudo python3 ~/robot_car_server.py

You should see:

  ==================================================
    Robot Car Server starting...
    GPIO:   enabled
    Camera: enabled
    Open your browser to: http://10.42.0.1
  ==================================================

On the connected laptop, navigate to http://10.42.0.1. The control
page will load with the live camera feed and D-pad controls.

----------------------------------------------------------------
Step 6 -- Auto-start the server on boot (optional)
----------------------------------------------------------------

Create a systemd service so the server starts automatically
when the Pi powers on:

  sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/robotcar.service

Paste the following (replace "pi" with your username if different):

  [Unit]
  Description=Robot Car Web Server
  After=network.target

  [Service]
  ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /home/pi/robot_car_server.py
  WorkingDirectory=/home/pi
  Restart=always
  User=root

  [Install]
  WantedBy=multi-user.target

Enable and start it:

  sudo systemctl enable robotcar
  sudo systemctl start robotcar


================================================================
USING THE WEB CONTROLLER
================================================================

Connect a laptop or phone to the Pi's hotspot, then open a browser
and go to http://10.42.0.1.

  Arrow Up / ^ button    Drive forward
  Arrow Down / v button  Drive backward
  Arrow Left / < button  Spin left
  Arrow Right / > button Spin right
  Spacebar / Stop button Stop immediately
  Speed slider           Adjust motor speed (20-100%)

NOTE: Hold a button or key to drive -- the car stops automatically
when you release it. The speed slider controls the PWM duty cycle
sent to the motor driver.


================================================================
TROUBLESHOOTING
================================================================

Hotspot not visible in WiFi list
  - Check the connection is active:
      nmcli connection show --active
  - Try bringing it up manually:
      sudo nmcli connection up RobotCar-Team1
  - Check for errors:
      sudo journalctl -u NetworkManager --since '5 min ago'
  - Make sure no other process is using wlan0:
      nmcli device status

Cannot reach 10.42.0.1 from laptop
  - Confirm you are connected to the Pi's hotspot, not school WiFi
  - The Flask server must be running:
      sudo python3 ~/robot_car_server.py
  - Confirm the Pi's IP:
      ip addr show wlan0  (look for 10.42.0.1)

Camera feed not showing
  - Check the ribbon cable is seated firmly in both the Pi CSI
    port and the camera module
  - Test with:
      libcamera-hello --timeout 2000
  - Confirm picamera2 is installed:
      python3 -c "import picamera2; print('OK')"
  - List detected cameras:
      libcamera-still --list-cameras

Motors not moving
  - Verify wiring against the GPIO pin numbers at the top of
    robot_car_server.py
  - The motor driver needs its own power supply (battery pack),
    not just the Pi's 5V rail
  - The ENA and ENB jumpers on the L298N must be removed so the
    Pi's PWM pins control motor speed
  - Quick test: run python3 and manually set a GPIO pin HIGH to
    confirm the motor responds

SSH dropped when hotspot was activated
  - This is expected -- wlan0 changes role from client to AP
  - Reconnect via Ethernet, or attach a keyboard and monitor
  - Next time, activate the hotspot from a local terminal session,
    not SSH over WiFi

Server error: Address already in use
  - Another process is using port 80. Find and stop it:
      sudo lsof -i :80
      sudo kill <PID shown above>


